Call a capability from your shell, host a short-lived peer, open a REPL, or
wire CapTP into an agent over MCP. The binary is the same library as the Zig
package, not a second stack. More recipes live in
[docs/cli.md](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/cli.md).

```sh
zig build
export PATH="$PWD/zig-out/bin:$PATH"
pancake help
```

## Round-trip

The demo greeter lives in the CLI adapter, not the library, and it is the same
greeter the README uses: verb `greet` plus one argument becomes `Hello, <arg>`.
`--publish` serves one partner and then exits, and `Ctrl+C` or `SIGTERM` stops
a waiting or serving `listen` cleanly with exit 0. Run terminal A first, then
paste its printed locator (the peer's dialable `ocapn://` address) into `LOC`
in terminal B. `init` mints the identity keys a peer signs its sessions with,
stored under `PANCAKE_STATE`; each terminal gets its own directory so it acts
as its own peer.

```sh
# terminal A
export PANCAKE_STATE=/tmp/pancake-a
pancake --json init
pancake --json ocapn listen --publish greeter
# {"ok":true,"locator":"ocapn://127.0.0.1%3A46659.tcp"}   (%3A is the : of host:port, URI-encoded)

# terminal B
export PANCAKE_STATE=/tmp/pancake-b
pancake --json init
LOC='ocapn://127.0.0.1%3A46659.tcp'   # paste A's locator
pancake --json ocapn call "$LOC" greeter greet world
#                         name──┘       │     └─ arg
#                         verb──────────┘
# {"ok":true,"value":"Hello, world"}
```

## REPL, MCP, and completion

The same binary serves agents over MCP and completes your shell. `pancake repl`
opens an interactive CapTP lab on a live session: the exports, imports, and
answers appear beside the prompt as you type the verbs, and typing `guide`
turns on a staged walkthrough. The REPL and `probe` want a live listener, so
those recipes stay in docs/cli.md.

```sh
pancake mcp --tools default                # agent control plane
eval "$(pancake completion bash)"          # tab completion
# probe / REPL: see docs/cli.md (need a fresh or long-lived listen)
```

When the round-trip works, the [demo apps]($link.page('examples')) are the
next act: the same verbs driving a chat room, a task board, and a capability
showcase across five languages.
